Here's a summary of the work.

## What changed

**`src/lib.rs`** — added the public function:
```rust
pub fn filter_by_tag<'a>(notes: &'a [Note], tag: &str) -> Vec<&'a Note> {
    notes.iter().filter(|n| n.tags.iter().any(|t| t == tag)).collect()
}
```
Exact-match on each note's `tags` (via `.any(|t| t == tag)`), order preserved by `iter()`, returns borrowed `&'a Note`.

**`src/main.rs`** — the `list` command now scans `args[2..]` for `--tag <TAG>`; when present it routes through `notekeep::filter_by_tag`, otherwise it lists all. Also added `Note` to the `use notekeep::{...}` import (it was previously missing and caused a compile error).

## Verified
- `cargo build` — succeeds.
- `cargo test` — the 2 existing unit tests pass (0 new tests, but none were broken).

## Not verified (and why)
My functional CLI test was inconclusive: every list (including the unfiltered one) came back empty. That's not a bug in the new code — it's a flaw in my test data. I wrote the `.note` files without the `\n---\n` separators that `store.rs` uses as its delimiter, so `load_all` never parsed any notes. The code path is correct; the input was malformed.

To verify end-to-end, create test files using the real on-disk format `id\n---\ntitle\n---\npinned\n---\nbody` (note: tags are extracted from the **body**, the 4th field). Example:
```
printf 'id1\n---\nhello #rust #world\n---\n0\n---\nhello #rust #world\n' > a.note
```
Then run `notekeep list --tag rust` against that data dir. I couldn't run that successfully before hitting the tool-call limit due to the format mistake, but the implementation itself compiles and is wired correctly.

